<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Izium</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Izyum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Izium is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> Oblast in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, on the Siverskyi Donets river. Founded as a fortress on Muscovy's southern frontier in the 1680s, it became the seat of one of the five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> regiments of Sloboda Ukraine. Russian forces occupied the heavily damaged city from April to September 2022, after which mass graves were found there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/izium.jpg" alt="Izium" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: D.Rovchak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Izum_Ascension_Cathedral_Panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The area around Izium has been inhabited since ancient times, and archaeologists have found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements. The land belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a>, and in 1111 the Battle of the Salnytsia River was fought on it, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-slavs">East Slavs</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir II Monomakh</a> and Sviatopolk II defeated a Cuman army. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> devastated the area in the 1240s. Muscovy built the Belgorod line (1635–1658) and the Izium line (1679–80) against Crimean Tatar raids, and in 1681 Colonel Donets of the Kharkiv Sloboda Cossack Regiment built a fortress named Izium on the site, generally regarded as the city's foundation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1685 until 1765 Izium was the seat of the Izium Regiment of Sloboda Ukraine, and its residents took part in the Bulavin Rebellion of 1707–1708. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> system was abolished in 1765 Izium received city status, and from 1780 it was the centre of Izyumsky Uyezd. By the 1880s it had factories making bricks, vodka, butter and wax and a population of 14,761. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donbas">Donbas</a> railway reached it in 1910, and in 1916 work began on the first optical glass plant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><p>Izium became part of the Ukrainian SSR after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a> of 1932–1933, 2,761 people from Izium are documented as having died. German forces occupied it from 24 June 1942, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> liberated it on 5 February 1943. Its population grew from about 12,000 in 1926 to 64,334 in January 1989, as a junction between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donbas">Donbas</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Izium lies on the Siverskyi Donets about 120 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> and is the administrative centre of Izium Raion, with 44,979 inhabitants in 2022. Its position on the rail and road link between Kharkiv and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donbas">Donbas</a> has long given it economic importance. Russian forces took the city on 1 April 2022, and Ukrainian forces recaptured it in September of that year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Izium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The region was organised into five Cossack regiments, Kharkiv, Sumy, Okhtyrka, <b>Izium</b> and Ostrogozhsk, each led by a colonel.</blockquote><a href="/state/sloboda-ukraine">Sloboda Ukraine</a></li></ul></section>
